  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f(x)$ be a non-negative continuous function such that the area bounded by the curve $y=f(x), x$-axis and the ordinates $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and $x=\\beta>\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is $\\left(\\beta \\sin \\beta+\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\cos \\beta+\\sqrt{2} \\beta\\right)$. Then $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Errue",
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}+\\sqrt{2}-1\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "gIgy4",
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}-\\sqrt{2}+1\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "FCqNE",
      "text": "\n$\\left(1-\\frac{\\pi}{4}-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "eO9BF",
      "text": "\n$\\left(1-\\frac{\\pi}{4}+\\sqrt{2}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given that $\\int_{\\pi / 4}^\\beta f(x) d x=\\beta \\sin \\beta+\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\cos \\beta+2 \\beta$\nDifferentiating w. r. t $\\beta$\n$f(\\beta)=\\beta \\cos \\beta+\\sin \\beta-\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\sin \\beta+\\sqrt{2}$\n$f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=\\left(1-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\sqrt{2}=1-\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\sqrt{2}$.",
    "image": null
